# ============================================================
# PAGE 1: What We Changed and Why
# ============================================================
heading('Methodological Approach: What We Built and Why Each Piece Matters', 2)

para('The original prototype used a GPT-style language model as a direct scorer, feeding it tweets and asking for compulsion and ragebait ratings on a numeric scale. This approach has three problems rooted in first principles. First, each call to a generative language model is stochastic. The same tweet fed through the same model on two different days will produce different scores, because the sampling process introduces randomness at the token level. There is no stable decision boundary and no way to reproduce a result. Second, a generative model produces a number, but that number has no calibrated relationship to probability. When a model says "7 out of 10 compulsive," there is no empirical basis for what that 7 means relative to a population. Third, you cannot inspect what drove the output. The model cannot be ablated or decomposed the way a classifier with known features can.')

para('We replaced this with a pipeline built on three independently defensible layers, each grounded in established methodology.')

para('The temporal phenotyping layer draws on digital phenotyping research from computational psychiatry (De Choudhury et al., 2013; Torous et al., 2016). Rather than analyzing what someone writes, it analyzes when and how often they write. We compute five features from posting timestamps alone: the Goh-Barab\u00e1si burstiness parameter, which distinguishes rapid-fire bursts from scheduled cadences; Shannon entropy of the hourly posting distribution, which reveals whether an account posts across all 24 hours or concentrates in business hours; the Hawkes process self-excitation ratio, which measures whether each post triggers further posting (a behavioral cascade); night posting intensity, which captures the share of posts made between midnight and 6am; and weekend-to-weekday posting ratio. These features are deterministic, reproducible, and grounded in the time-series and complex-systems literature.')

para('The text classification layer uses twelve models in parallel. Six are off-the-shelf models from the CardiffNLP group, trained on 124 million tweets, measuring sentiment, emotion, offensiveness, irony, hate speech, and toxicity. These are well-validated community standards. The other six we trained ourselves on dimensions the standard models miss entirely: ragebait (content designed to provoke), tribal signaling (us-versus-them framing), performative outrage (theatrical anger versus genuine concern), epistemic manipulation (cherry-picking and straw-manning), engagement baiting (polls, rhetorical questions, calls to action), and agency language (whether the poster speaks as an agent or a victim). Training labels were generated by a large language model applied to 4,121 tweets from 14 accounts across both parties, then distilled into lightweight SetFit classifiers that run in milliseconds without any API call.')

para('The validation layer is where we departed most from the prototype. We constructed ground truth labels for 32 accounts using evidence that is entirely independent of the temporal features we classify on: staff testimony from published books, documented incidents, health and demographic factors, and role-based knowledge of Senate communications structures. We deliberately included 19 ambiguous accounts (labeled 0.3 to 0.7) to force the model to produce honest middle-range probabilities rather than slamming everything to zero or one. The resulting logistic regression achieves AUC=0.933 and correlates at Spearman r=0.912 with these independent labels. When the model says a senator has a 70% probability of compulsive posting, that number means something grounded in external evidence, not in a circular rediscovery of its own features.')

doc.add_page_break()

# ============================================================
# PAGE 2: Findings
# ============================================================
heading('Key Findings', 2)

para('Senator Mike Lee', bold=True)
para('Senator Lee\u2019s @BasedMikeLee account is the most extreme case in our dataset by a wide margin. Over 3.5 years he posted 49,415 tweets, averaging one every 38 minutes around the clock. Forty percent of his posts occurred between midnight and 6am. His Hawkes self-excitation ratio of 0.984 means that virtually every tweet he posts triggers additional tweets, a self-sustaining cascade that is the temporal signature of a compulsive engagement loop. His inter-tweet gap distribution follows a power law rather than a normal or exponential distribution, a pattern associated in the complex-systems literature with bursty, addiction-like behavior rather than deliberate communication. What makes Lee\u2019s case especially striking is the disconnect between what standard toxicity classifiers see and what is actually happening. CardiffNLP models score his overall virulence at 19.2 out of 100, because he rarely uses overtly hateful or abusive language. But our custom classifiers reveal that 57.7% of his tweets employ tribal us-versus-them framing, 52.3% contain performative outrage, and 47.8% are structured as ragebait. He is not toxic in the way those models are trained to detect. He is provocative in a specifically political register that existing tools are blind to. His posting trajectory is escalating: weekly volume grew from 73 tweets per week in early 2022 to over 420 per week by late 2025, with ten cusp catastrophe events (sudden collapses followed by rebound surges) detected in the time series. The overall picture is consistent with someone whose relationship to the platform has become compulsive rather than strategic.')

para('Party Trends', bold=True)
para('Compulsion probabilities did not differ significantly by party (\u03c7\u00b2=0.15, p>0.05). Republican senators averaged P=0.436, Democrats P=0.398. Both parties have members at both extremes. Among Republicans, Rubio (0.968), Cornyn (0.947), Hagerty (0.921), Blackburn (0.884), and Cruz (0.869) scored highest. The Republican compulsive cluster tends to include senators with confrontational public personas who use Twitter as a primary vehicle for base mobilization. Among Democrats, Murphy (0.959), Blumenthal (0.874), Coons (0.839), and Schumer (0.740) scored highest. The Democratic compulsive cluster skews toward senators who use the platform for policy advocacy and real-time political commentary, with Murphy in particular having publicly discussed his own heavy social media engagement.')

para('Notable Individuals', bold=True)
para('At the strategic end, McConnell (0.057) and Klobuchar (0.053) represent the prototypical staff-managed accounts: posting concentrated in business hours, near-zero night activity, and regular inter-post intervals consistent with scheduling tools. Feinstein (0.079), who was 88 to 90 during the study period with documented cognitive decline, is effectively a control case confirming that our model identifies obvious staff management. Among Republicans, Shelby (0.014) is the single most strategic account, consistent with his age (87 to 88) and retirement in January 2023. Lisa Murkowski (0.836) is a notable outlier within the Republican caucus, scoring highly compulsive despite her reputation as a moderate. Her temporal signatures suggest personal, always-on engagement that does not align with the combative style of other high-compulsion Republicans. In the House, preliminary analysis of 30 members found Rep. Joaquin Castro (D-TX, 0.843) and Rep. Thomas Massie (R-KY, 0.841) as the highest scorers, with Massie\u2019s 24% night posting share consistent with his reputation for personally managing his social media presence.')

para('Summary', bold=True)
para('The pipeline produces calibrated, reproducible compulsion estimates that are validated against external evidence and grounded in the digital phenotyping and behavioral addiction literature. The full manuscript is formatted for submission to the International Journal of Drug Policy. The model, data, and code are archived on HuggingFace under private repositories. I am reaching out to Wayne Kepner and his co-authors for additional feedback on the Recovery Viability Theory integration before final submission.')

